### Cleanup a user's custom policies

Delete the specified user's **custom policies** in the specified system (temporary policies excluded). Executes synchronously and returns the result so that the caller can verify the actual cleanup result.

Before cleanup, use `subjects/user/{subject_id}/policies/` to list the user's custom policies and their `policy_id` values, which can be used as `policy_ids` here; after cleanup, verify the records via the audit events API (`audits/events/`, `type=user.policy.delete`, `username` is the cleaned user).

`action_id` and `policy_ids` are mutually exclusive; exactly one of them must be passed:

- Only `action_id`: clean up the user's policies of that action
- Only `policy_ids`: clean up only the specified policies (SaaS-side policy IDs, at most 200)
- Neither or both: a validation error is returned

#### Parameters

| Field | Type | Location | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| username | string | body | yes | Username |
| system_id | string | body | yes | System ID |
| action_id | string | body | no | Clean up only the policies of this action, mutually exclusive with policy_ids |
| policy_ids | array[int] | body | no | IDs of the policies to clean up (SaaS-side), mutually exclusive with action_id, at most 200 |

#### Request

```json
{
  "username": "zhangsan",
  "system_id": "bk_cmdb",
  "action_id": "host_delete"
}
```

#### Response

> Status: 200 OK

```json
{
  "code": 0,
  "message": "OK",
  "data": {
    "removed": [123],
    "failed": [456]
  }
}
```

Field notes:

- `removed`: IDs of the policies deleted successfully
- `failed`: IDs of the policies whose deletion failed (after the whole batch is rolled back, all valid policy IDs are included in this field)
